    <fr:title text="The peeling induction">The peeling induction</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
    <fr:meta name="area">frontier</fr:meta>
    <fr:meta name="thread">ns</fr:meta>
    <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
    <fr:meta name="provenance">scratch_nineteenth.md item 15(f); instruments mb_fiber, mb_peel, mb_peelpair, mb_s0, mb_i2, mb_q0, mb_grade, mb_k3, mb_s0s.</fr:meta>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0045/" title="(NS): the strict/centralizer inequality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0045/" display-uri="ffi-0045" type="local">(NS)</fr:link> reduces to two one-peel local comparisons: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \text {(C1)} \quad  \mathrm {ex}_{s} \le  \mathrm {ex}_{z},
  \qquad 
  \text {(C2)} \quad  q_{s} \le  q_{z}.
]]></fr:tex></html:p>
    <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> Exact one-peel ladders
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N(\text {big}) = N(\text {small}) + \mathrm {ex} + q]]></fr:tex>: a zero-<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[e]]></fr:tex> row makes
the pairing factor through the restriction <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B \to  B_0]]></fr:tex>, and
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[N(\text {big}) \cap  \text {over-}R_0 = W]]></fr:tex> exactly. The excess obeys the
automatic cap
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {ex} \le  \operatorname {codim} \operatorname {res} B]]></fr:tex> and, precisely, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \mathrm {ex} = \operatorname {codim} - \dim ((S\text {-set} + \operatorname {res} B)/\operatorname {res} B),
]]></fr:tex>
so (C1) is equivalent to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S_0 \subseteq  S_0^{s} + \operatorname {res} B]]></fr:tex>. The kernel
moves in lockstep, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[dN_z = dN_w = dN_s]]></fr:tex>, measured at every peel, and
the 3-block case of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0043/" title="Lemma V" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0043/" display-uri="ffi-0043" type="local">Lemma V</fr:link> is the base.</html:p>
    <html:p><html:strong>Status.</html:strong> Both comparisons hold with equality at all 28 measured peels.
The saturation shortcut (I1) — <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {ex}_{z} = \operatorname {codim} \operatorname {res} B]]></fr:tex> — holds at
flank length 2 but is <html:strong>refuted</html:strong> at length <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\ge  3]]></fr:tex> (mb_k3),
exactly where <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S_0 \not \subseteq  \operatorname {res} B]]></fr:tex>; the equivalence (I1)
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\iff ]]></fr:tex> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S_0 \subseteq  \operatorname {res} B]]></fr:tex> does stand. The rigidity calculus
gives <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S_0 = \operatorname {rig}(\Xi _A)]]></fr:tex>, tight 20/20 by a tangent argument, with
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x \cdot  \operatorname {soc} R_0 = 0]]></fr:tex> proven by corner probes; the
dual-symmetry and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Q_0]]></fr:tex>-cap routes are refuted.</html:p>
    <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0043/" title="Lemma V" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0043/" display-uri="ffi-0043" type="local">Lemma V</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0045/" title="(NS): the strict/centralizer inequality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0045/" display-uri="ffi-0045" type="local">(NS): the strict/centralizer inequality</fr:link>.</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
